WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.400
You've seen that supervised machine&nbsp;
learning algorithms can classify your data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.800
Unsupervised machine learning&nbsp;
algorithms can cluster your data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.480
Now let's look at an entirely different set of&nbsp;
algorithms based on conditional probability.&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.320
Say you're looking to see how one thing impacts&nbsp;
the probability of another thing happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.160
The most popular algorithm for this type of&nbsp;
analysis is called the Bayesian algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:38.160
based on the Bayes' theory of statistics. Naive&nbsp;
Bayes is one of the most popular Bayesian machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.000
learning algorithms. It's called naive because it&nbsp;
assumes that all of the predictors are independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.840
from one another. Naive Bayes is mostly used&nbsp;
for binary or multiclass classification.&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:57.360
Let's go back to our animal shelter in Chicago.&nbsp;
Imagine that we wanted to classify all the dogs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.920
in the shelter based on their different&nbsp;
breeds. Remember that there are hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:07.440
different dog breeds. On top of that, most&nbsp;
dogs would be a mixture of several breeds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.240
Let's look at this problem using a&nbsp;
naive Bayes machine learning algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:19.600
To start, let's create three classes of dog&nbsp;
breeds. We'll use terrier, hounds, and sport dogs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.920
Now, for each of these classes, we'll have&nbsp;
three predictors. Let's use hair length, height,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:32.800
and weight. Remember that some of the predictors&nbsp;
will be closely autocorrelated. A tall dog is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.560
likely to be heavier. But naive Bayes considers&nbsp;
each one of these predictors independently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.560
Remember, that's why it's called naive.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.520
Once you have your classes and predictors set up,&nbsp;
then the naive Bayes machine learning algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.560
starts with something called class predictor&nbsp;
probability. This is when it looks at each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.360
the independent predictors and tries to create a&nbsp;
probability that the dog belongs in each class.&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.040
Let's see what happens when you're&nbsp;
trying to identify an unknown dog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.200
The first predictor you look at is hair length.&nbsp;
The machine learning algorithm can check the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.800
probability of a dog with this hair length&nbsp;
belonging to any of the three classes. It finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.720
that a dog with this hair length has a 40% chance&nbsp;
of being a terrier, a 10% chance of being a hound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.240
and a 50% chance that it's a sport dog. The&nbsp;
next thing you check is the unknown dog's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.760
height. Again, naive Bayes doesn't correlate&nbsp;
the dog's hair length with the dog's height.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.920
It looks at this predictor independently and tries&nbsp;
to calculate the probability that this unknown dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:44.320
will belong to each class. So, again, it looks&nbsp;
at the training data and finds that there's a 20%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.080
chance that it's a terrier, a 10% chance that it's&nbsp;
a hound, and a 70% chance that it's a sport dog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.920
The final thing you want to check&nbsp;
is the unknown dog's weight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.640
This might seem like a strange predictor&nbsp;
because it's closely related to height.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.720
But remember, the naive Bayes is evaluating the&nbsp;
probability of each predictor independently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.760
It looks at the training data and finds&nbsp;
there's a 10% chance it's a terrier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.400
a 5% chance that it's a hound, and&nbsp;
an 85% chance that it's a sport dog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:19.200
You now have this table with the unknown&nbsp;
dog's class predictor probability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:26.160
If you look at it, you can see that the dog is&nbsp;
probably a sport dog. But remember, most machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.120
learning problems are dealing with terabytes&nbsp;
or even petabytes of dog data and is trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.080
classify millions of unknown dogs.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.320
To classify your dog, you might want to use&nbsp;
something called the weighted multiplication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.520
function. Since this is a weighted function, the&nbsp;
first thing you want to do is create a weight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.840
It's here you'll decide which one of&nbsp;
your predictors is the most predictive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.600
You can pick this weight by looking at the&nbsp;
training data. You can also tweak it if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.880
improves your accuracy. Here we'll use a weight&nbsp;
of three for hair and two for weight and height.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.400
For the weighted multiplication function, take&nbsp;
the predictor and multiply it by your weight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.400
When you add up the predictors for&nbsp;
hair length, height, and weight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.920
you can see that the second unknown&nbsp;
dog is most likely a sport dog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:23.920
It's less likely that it's a terrier&nbsp;
and very unlikely that it's a hound.

